COUNTY COUNSEL IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS
MEASURE TO FUND SAN DIEGO COUNTY HEALTH & SAFETY
SERVICES
This measure was placed on the ballot by a petition signed by the requisite
number of voters.
This measure, if approved by a majority vote of qualified electors, would
impose a transaction and use tax of a half cent per dollar on the sale of all
tangible personal property in the County of San Diego, in both incorporated
and unincorporated areas. The tax would be administered similarly to
collection of other state and local sales taxes.
The tax is subject to certain specified exemptions and exclusions that are
generally consistent with state and locally imposed sales taxes. The sales
tax would remain in effect until repealed by voters. It is estimated that the
sales tax would generate approximately $400,000,000 to $450,000,000 per
year.
This measure would establish a Protect San Diego County’s Health and
Safety Fund for revenues generated. Funds would only be used for specified
purposes, including efforts to stop sewage pollution in the Tijuana River
Valley, wildfire prevention and emergency response, access to healthcare,
food assistance, and childhood development services, and authorized
administrative expenses. The measure would allocate funding as follows:
• Stopping sewage pollution in the Tijuana River Valley: 22.5%
• Public safety services and wildfire prevention: 17.5%
• Healthcare, childcare, and nutrition services: 60%
The measure would establish a twelve-member oversight committee, which
would audit how funds are spent to ensure compliance with the measure’s
requirements. The Board of Supervisors for the County of San Diego would
appoint eleven of the twelve members of this committee, who would be
required to have certain qualifications specified in the measure. The twelfth
member of this committee would be the County of San Diego Auditor. The
measure would also prohibit the use of funds for such things as elected
official or County employee salaries, lobbyist contracts, or government office
renovations. The measure would require public disclosure of all spending.
A YES vote would impose a half-cent sales tax for use to clean-up
sewage pollution in the Tijuana River Valley, public safety, wildfire
prevention, healthcare, childcare and nutrition services.
A NO vote would reject a half-cent sales tax for use to clean-up sewage
pollution in the Tijuana River Valley, public safety, wildfire prevention,
healthcare, childcare and nutrition services.
