RAMONA MUNICIPAL WATER DISTRICT
MEASURE P
PR-W00001-1-ENG
(This measure will appear on the ballot in the following form.)
MEASURE P
AMENDMENT OF PROPOSITION ‘NN’ TO MODERNIZE THE ORDINANCE.
Shall the voters of the Ramona Municipal Water District adopt a measure
amending Proposition ‘NN’ by (1) exempting grant funding and donations from the
calculation of authorized expenditure limit; (2) increasing the expenditure limit for
new projects from $1,000,000 to $10,000,000 where no debt is incurred; and
(3) establishing annual adjustments to the expenditure limit based on the San
Diego Area Consumer Price Index beginning January 1, 2028?
This measure requires Simple Majority (50%+1) of those voting on the measure.
DISTRICT COUNSEL IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS
The Board of Directors for the Ramona Municipal Water District (“District”)
placed this measure on the ballot. If approved by a majority of the votes cast,
this measure will amend Proposition “NN,” which established Ordinance No.
108 (Chapter 99.04 of the District’s Regulatory Code). Because Proposition
NN was a citizens’ initiative, it can only be repealed or amended by a majority
vote.
Why A Vote Is Required:
Proposition NN’s stated purpose was to require public approval of the
District’s “expenditures for major projects.” However, Proposition NN
imposed an “authorized debt limit,” rather than an “authorized expenditure
limit.” The initial limit was set at $1,000,000 but allowed for annual
adjustments based on the San Diego Area Consumer Price Index (CPI) for
all Urban Consumers. Based on those adjustments, the current limit is
approximately $3,300,000. For the past 38 years, the District has treated the
debt limit as an expenditure limit.
Effects of the Measure:
The purpose of this measure is to amend Proposition NN to (1) exempt grant
funding and donations from expenditure and debt limit calculations;
(2) establish an “expenditure limit,” in the initial amount of $10,000,000, with
CPI adjustments beginning January 1, 2028; and (3) keep debt for major
projects limited to the amounts initially established by Proposition NN as
adjusted annually since 1989, currently approximately $3,300,000.
The proposed measure also makes changes to Proposition NN that would
clarify the meaning of “expenditure limits” and “debt limits,” without
increasing the current limit on borrowing for projects.
What A “Yes” Or “No” Vote Means:
A “yes” vote means the following: (1) grant funding and donations would be
exempt from expenditure and debt limit calculations; (2) an “expenditure
limit,” will be established in the initial amount of $10,000,000, with CPI
adjustments beginning January 1, 2028; and (3) debt for major projects will
be limited to the amounts initially established by Proposition NN as adjusted
annually since 1989, currently approximately $3,300,000.
A “no” vote means that Proposition NN would remain unchanged with no
expenditure limit specified and the current debt limit of approximately
$3,300,000 as adjusted annually since 1989 would remain in place.
Steven E. Boehmer, District General Counsel
The above statement is an impartial analysis of Ordinance or
Measure P. If you desire a copy of the ordinance or measure, please
call the district elections official’s office at 858-505-7260 and a copy
will be mailed at no cost to you.
ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF MEASURE P
In 1988, Ramona Municipal Water District voters adopted Proposition NN
which prohibits the District from spending more than $1 million for new
capital projects, property acquisitions and/or any improvements to real
property unless first approved by voters. Proposition NN included an
escalation factor, and the current limit in 2026 is approximately $3.3 million.
The limit includes all sources of funds, including grants.
We are asking voters to modernize Proposition NN to update the limit to
$10 million and exempt grants while retaining the community’s right to vote
on major projects and when the District incurs debt over $3.3 million.
Construction costs have increased faster than inflation
Construction costs have risen faster than inflation. In 1988, voters could not
anticipate 21st century environmental, permitting, and other regulations that
would exist in 2026. A replacement pipeline that was estimated to cost
$1.6 million in 1988 was completed in 2023 for $5.6 million.
Allows District to maximize grant and donation funds
Grants and donations help reduce pressure on local ratepayers and are
important to help us protect affordability. These funds were not exempted.
Grants move quickly and have a firm deadline to complete projects. Holding
an election prior to entering a grant contract means that the District has lost
opportunities for grants.
Measure P maintains the voter authority established in 1988
This measure retains the community’s right to vote on major projects and on
debt incurred while modernizing the language to take advantage of grant
funding and to adjust the threshold for 21st century costs.
Modernizing Proposition NN with Measure P makes sense. It maintains the
voter’s role in authorizing District debt, while allowing the District to complete
timely, necessary projects and take advantage of limited grant funding.
Please vote yes on Measure P. For more information, visit
UpdatePropNN.org.
